---
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
import AffiliateDisclosure from '../../components/AffiliateDisclosure.astro';
import AppCTA from '../../components/AppCTA.astro';

const faqs = [
  {
    q: 'What inseam should I wear if I am 5\'5"?',
    a: 'Most people around 5\'5" land in the 28 to 30 inch range for a full-length jean, but rise and shoe choice shift the target. A higher rise uses up more length above the crotch seam, and wearing heels or chunky sneakers lets you go an inch or so longer than flats.',
  },
  {
    q: 'How do I measure the inseam on jeans I already own?',
    a: 'Take a pair that fits the way you like, lay it flat with the legs smoothed out, and run a tape measure along the inner leg seam from the crotch seam down to the bottom of the hem. That number is the inseam; use it as your target when shopping.',
  },
  {
    q: 'What is the difference between petite, regular, and tall jeans?',
    a: 'They are cut for different heights, not different widths. Petite jeans typically run about 2 inches shorter in the inseam than regular, and tall runs about 2 inches longer. The rise and knee placement are scaled too, so proportions sit correctly, not just hem length.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Should cropped jeans have a shorter inseam?',
    a: 'Yes. Cropped styles are designed to end above the ankle, so their inseams typically run about 2 to 4 inches shorter than a full-length jean in the same size. If a crop is labeled with its inseam, compare it to your full-length number to picture where it will hit on your leg.',
  },
];
const faqJsonLd = JSON.stringify({
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'FAQPage',
  mainEntity: faqs.map((f) => ({ '@type': 'Question', name: f.q, acceptedAnswer: { '@type': 'Answer', text: f.a } })),
});
---

<Base
  title="Jeans Inseam & Length Guide: Inseam by Height"
  description="Inseam is the inner-leg length from crotch seam to hem. See typical inseams by height, petite vs tall lengths, and how hem style changes the target."
  ogType="article"
>
  <article class="mx-auto max-w-article px-5 py-16">
    <p class="text-sm font-semibold uppercase tracking-wide text-plum">Size Guide</p>
    <h1 class="mt-2 font-display text-4xl font-semibold leading-tight tracking-tight md:text-5xl">
      Jeans Inseam &amp; Length Guide: Find Your Inseam by Height
    </h1>

    <AffiliateDisclosure />

    <p class="mt-8 leading-relaxed text-ink/85">
      Waist size gets all the attention, but the inseam is what decides whether jeans look tailored or borrowed.
      Too long and the fabric pools at your ankles; too short and a full-length jean reads as an accidental crop.
      The good news is that inseam is the easiest denim measurement to pin down: one number, measured once, works
      across nearly every brand. Here's what the inseam actually measures, how to find yours without a fitting
      room, and how height, rise, and hem style all pull on that number.
    </p>

    <h2 class="mt-12 font-display text-2xl font-semibold tracking-tight">What the inseam actually is</h2>
    <p class="mt-3 leading-relaxed text-ink/85">
      The inseam is the length of the inner leg seam, running from the point where the four seams meet at the
      crotch down to the bottom edge of the hem. It is a garment measurement, not a body measurement, which is why it's so
      reliable: a 30-inch inseam is 30 inches of fabric no matter who makes the jeans. Men's jeans usually state
      it outright (a 32×32 is a 32-inch waist with a 32-inch inseam), while women's jeans more often hide it behind
      labels like "regular" or "long", but the number is almost always listed in the product details.
    </p>

    <h2 class="mt-12 font-display text-2xl font-semibold tracking-tight">How to measure your inseam</h2>
    <p class="mt-3 leading-relaxed text-ink/85">
      The most accurate method starts with denim you already trust. For measuring your body directly (waist, hip,
      and thigh included), our
      <a href="/how-to-measure/" class="font-semibold text-plum underline decoration-plum/40 underline-offset-2 hover:decoration-plum">measuring guide</a>
      walks through the full routine.
    </p>
    <ul class="mt-4 list-disc space-y-2 pl-5 text-ink/85">
      <li>
        <strong>Grab a well-fitting pair.</strong> Pick jeans whose length you'd happily clone, in a similar rise
        to what you're shopping for.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Lay them flat.</strong> Smooth each leg out on a hard surface so there are no ripples or folds along
        the inner seam.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Measure crotch seam to hem.</strong> Run the tape along the inside leg seam from the crotch point to
        the bottom of the hem. Measure both legs and split the difference if they disagree slightly.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>No good pair on hand?</strong> Stand against a wall in bare feet, have someone measure from the top
        of your inner thigh to the floor, then subtract roughly an inch (more if you like a cropped finish, less if
        you want stacking over shoes).
      </li>
    </ul>

    <h2 class="mt-12 font-display text-2xl font-semibold tracking-tight">Typical inseam by height</h2>
    <p class="mt-3 leading-relaxed text-ink/85">
      The table below shows <strong>typical</strong> full-length inseam ranges by height. Treat it as a starting
      bracket, not a rule: a high rise sits farther up your waist and effectively needs less inseam, a low rise
      needs more, and heel-wearers can comfortably carry an extra inch that flats-and-sneakers people can't.
    </p>
    <div class="mt-3 overflow-x-auto rounded-xl border border-ink/10">
      <table class="w-full border-collapse bg-white/70 text-sm" style="min-width:560px">
        <thead>
          <tr class="bg-plum text-left text-white">
            <th class="px-4 py-3 font-semibold">Height</th>
            <th class="px-4 py-3 font-semibold">Typical full-length inseam</th>
            <th class="px-4 py-3 font-semibold">Notes</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr class="border-t border-ink/10">
            <td class="px-4 py-3 font-semibold">5'0"–5'3"</td>
            <td class="px-4 py-3 text-smoke">26–28"</td>
            <td class="px-4 py-3 text-smoke">Petite ranges are usually the better hunting ground</td>
          </tr>
          <tr class="border-t border-ink/10">
            <td class="px-4 py-3 font-semibold">5'4"–5'7"</td>
            <td class="px-4 py-3 text-smoke">28–30"</td>
            <td class="px-4 py-3 text-smoke">Most "regular" inseams are cut around this bracket</td>
          </tr>
          <tr class="border-t border-ink/10">
            <td class="px-4 py-3 font-semibold">5'8"–5'11"</td>
            <td class="px-4 py-3 text-smoke">30–32"</td>
            <td class="px-4 py-3 text-smoke">Top of regular, bottom of tall, so check both</td>
          </tr>
          <tr class="border-t border-ink/10">
            <td class="px-4 py-3 font-semibold">6'0" and up</td>
            <td class="px-4 py-3 text-smoke">32–34"</td>
            <td class="px-4 py-3 text-smoke">Look for dedicated tall or 34"+ inseam options</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p class="mt-3 leading-relaxed text-ink/85">
      Height alone doesn't settle it because leg-to-torso proportion varies widely — two people the same height can
      easily need inseams two inches apart. That's the whole argument for measuring a favorite pair instead of
      guessing from a chart.
    </p>

    <h2 class="mt-12 font-display text-2xl font-semibold tracking-tight">Petite, regular, and tall inseams</h2>
    <p class="mt-3 leading-relaxed text-ink/85">
      When a brand offers length variants, the jump between them is typically about two inches of inseam: petite
      runs roughly 2" shorter than regular, and tall roughly 2" longer. But length labels change more than the hem.
      Petite cuts also lower the rise and raise the knee break so the whole jean is proportioned for a shorter
      frame, and tall cuts do the reverse. If you've been hemming regular jeans and the knees still land in the
      wrong place, the length category is the real fix; our
      <a href="/blog/petite-vs-regular-vs-tall/" class="font-semibold text-plum underline decoration-plum/40 underline-offset-2 hover:decoration-plum">petite vs regular vs tall explainer</a>
      digs into exactly what shifts between the three. Brands like Levi's publish inseam options per style, which we
      break down in our
      <a href="/size-guides/levis-size-chart-fit-guide/" class="font-semibold text-plum underline decoration-plum/40 underline-offset-2 hover:decoration-plum">Levi's size chart and fit guide</a>.
    </p>

    <h2 class="mt-12 font-display text-2xl font-semibold tracking-tight">How hem style changes your target inseam</h2>
    <p class="mt-3 leading-relaxed text-ink/85">
      Your "true" inseam is really your <em>full-length</em> inseam, where the hem grazes the top of your foot.
      Every other hem style is defined relative to that number, so once you know it, you can shop any silhouette
      by simple subtraction.
    </p>
    <ul class="mt-4 list-disc space-y-2 pl-5 text-ink/85">
      <li>
        <strong>Full length:</strong> hem reaches the top of the foot, often with a slight break. This is your
        measured inseam, or up to an inch longer if you like stacking on straight and slim cuts.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Ankle length:</strong> hem finishes right at the ankle bone, typically 1–2" shorter than your
        full-length number. Flattering with loafers, flats, and low sneakers.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Cropped:</strong> ends visibly above the ankle, usually 2–4" shorter than full length. Wide-leg
        crops in particular depend on hitting the intended spot on your calf, so check the stated inseam.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Flares and bootcuts:</strong> the exception that runs long — these are designed to cover most of
        the shoe, so aim for the top of your full-length range and hem to the shoes you'll actually wear.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p class="mt-3 leading-relaxed text-ink/85">
      One last wrinkle: some regions size denim by centimeters rather than inches, and vanity-sized waists muddy
      cross-brand comparisons further. Our
      <a href="/size-guides/international-size-conversion/" class="font-semibold text-plum underline decoration-plum/40 underline-offset-2 hover:decoration-plum">international size conversion charts</a>
      translate the waist numbers, and the
      <a href="/size-guides/gap-size-chart-fit-guide/" class="font-semibold text-plum underline decoration-plum/40 underline-offset-2 hover:decoration-plum">Gap fit guide</a>
      covers two of the most common denim wardrobes in US closets.
    </p>

    <h2 class="mt-12 font-display text-2xl font-semibold tracking-tight">Inseam FAQ</h2>
    <div class="mt-4 space-y-5">
      {faqs.map((f) => (
        <div class="rounded-xl border border-ink/10 bg-white/60 p-5">
          <h3 class="font-semibold text-ink">{f.q}</h3>
          <p class="mt-2 leading-relaxed text-ink/85">{f.a}</p>
        </div>
      ))}
    </div>

    <h2 class="mt-12 font-display text-2xl font-semibold tracking-tight">Keep reading</h2>
    <ul class="mt-4 list-disc space-y-2 pl-5">
      <li>
        <a href="/size-guides/levis-size-chart-fit-guide/" class="font-semibold text-plum underline decoration-plum/40 underline-offset-2 hover:decoration-plum">Levi's size chart &amp; fit guide</a>
      </li>
      <li>
        <a href="/blog/petite-vs-regular-vs-tall/" class="font-semibold text-plum underline decoration-plum/40 underline-offset-2 hover:decoration-plum">Petite vs regular vs tall: what actually changes</a>
      </li>
      <li>
        <a href="/size-guides/gap-size-chart-fit-guide/" class="font-semibold text-plum underline decoration-plum/40 underline-offset-2 hover:decoration-plum">Gap size chart &amp; fit guide</a>
      </li>
      <li>
        <a href="/how-to-measure/" class="font-semibold text-plum underline decoration-plum/40 underline-offset-2 hover:decoration-plum">How to measure yourself at home</a>
      </li>
    </ul>

    <AppCTA />
  </article>

  <script type="application/ld+json" set:html={faqJsonLd} is:inline />
</Base>
